<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tchaikovsky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky was a Russian composer of the nineteenth century, known for symphonies, concertos, operas and ballets such as Swan Lake and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-nutcracker">The Nutcracker</a>. He was a central figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s cultural flowering. His music helped spread Russian cultural influence internationally.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky.jpg" alt="Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky" width="301" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Émile Reutlinger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tchaikovsky_by_Reutlinger_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tchaikovsky was born on 7 May 1840 in Votkinsk, a small town in Vyatka Governorate near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kama-river">Kama River</a>. His father, Ilya Petrovich Tchaikovsky, was a lieutenant colonel and engineer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Mines and managed the Ironworks in Kamsko-Votkinsk. Although musically precocious, he was educated for a career as a civil servant, since there was little opportunity for a musical career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and no institutional music education. On 10 June 1859, aged 19, he graduated as a titular counselor, a low rung on the civil service ladder, and spent three years in the Ministry of Justice. He then entered the new Saint Petersburg Conservatory, from which he graduated in 1865.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Western-oriented teaching he received at the conservatory set him apart from the nationalist composers of The Five, Mily Balakirev, César Cui, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Modest Mussorgsky</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-rimsky-korsakov">Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</a> and Alexander Borodin, who thought conservatories unnecessary. Tchaikovsky and his fellow students were caught between them and Anton Rubinstein, who defended professional training. His successes came infrequently and with great effort, and he was lifelong sensitive to criticism; Nikolai Rubinstein attacked the First Piano Concerto in private fits of rage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-von-bulow">Hans von Bülow</a> premiered it and championed other works. From 1867 the publication of his songs and piano music for the home market helped his popularity.</p><p>In March 1884 Emperor Alexander III conferred on him the Order of Saint Vladimir, which carried hereditary nobility and a personal audience with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>. In 1885 the emperor requested a new production of Eugene Onegin at the Bolshoi Kamenny Theatre, signalling that his music was replacing Italian opera as the official imperial art, and awarded him a lifetime annual pension of 3,000 rubles. In his last years he grew at ease with the Belyayev circle around Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a>. In 1892 he was voted a member of the Académie des Beaux-Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in 1893 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> gave him an honorary Doctor of Music degree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tchaikovsky died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> on 6 November 1893, aged 53, and was interred in Tikhvin Cemetery at the Alexander Nevsky Monastery. His death is attributed to cholera, caused by drinking unboiled water at a local restaurant. In the 1980s there was academic speculation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> that he killed himself, and Roland John Wiley wrote that we do not know how he died and may never find out. His music has remained popular, though critics were initially divided: some Russians thought it insufficiently Russian, while some Europeans praised it for offering more than exoticism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian cultural influence spread through the Orthodox Church, the Russian language imposed on subject peoples from Poles to Kazakhs, and the literature and music of the nineteenth century, represented by Pushkin, Tolstoy, Dostoevsky, <b>Tchaikovsky</b> and Mussorgsky.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In music the Romantic style lived on through Richard Wagner, Johannes Brahms and <b>Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</b> to the end of the century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nutcracker, the ballet by <b>Tchaikovsky</b> whose plot is set on Christmas Eve, was first performed on 18 December 1892 in Saint Petersburg and was not deemed a success; only since the late 1960s has it been danced by many companies, especially in North America.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Liszt, Saint-Saëns, Mahler, Mussorgsky, <b>Tchaikovsky</b> and Rachmaninoff, in more than a dozen works, quoted it, and it has been used in many film scores and popular works, from the score for Metropolis (1927) and Citizen Kane (1941) to The Shining (1980) and The Lion King.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>…the students' ball at the Vienna Hofburg on 24 February 1862, Suppé in the overture of his operetta Flotte Bursche (1863), Liszt in a paraphrase and a humoresque (1870), <b>Tchaikovsky</b> in an arrangement for male chorus (1874), and Brahms in the full-orchestra close of his Academic Festival Overture.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tchaikovsky</b> quoted it in the 1812 Overture, the Marche Slave, his overture on the Danish national anthem and the Festival Coronation March.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-tsar">God Save the Tsar!</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Votkinsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Malaya Morskaya Street, 13, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.05,54&amp;year=1874" aria-label="Votkinsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5713 229 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6163 499h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6163 499h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57.05,54&amp;year=1874">What was here in 1874? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
